Title: "Dimension and Duality"
Id: "b20dd8da-14fc-43f0-b75e-31c74de540cd"


Text: "A basis translates an abstract vector space into a concrete coordinate
       space, yet the number of vectors in any basis is an intrinsic invariant
       of the space itself: its *dimension*. Dimension characterizes vector
       spaces up to isomorphism, quantifying their topological and algebraic
       degrees of freedom.

       Complementary to the vectors themselves is the *dual space*, which
       collects all linear measurements (functionals) on the space. Duality
       reverses arrows: a linear map $T : V \to W$ induces a transpose map
       $T^* : W^* \to V^*$ acting on measurements. Through annihilators, dual
       bases, and the bidual isomorphism, duality reveals the geometric symmetry
       underpinning linear systems and orthogonality."
Id: "dea851d9-f757-4783-a464-72424c40781f"


SectionTitle: "Bases and Dimension"
Id: "ac3e313d-5cfb-4d82-ab0a-d2249dc10e7d"


Text: "A basis is a subset $B \subseteq V$ that is simultaneously linearly
       independent and spans $V$. Equivalently, every vector $v \in V$ admits a
       *unique* expression as a finite linear combination of basis vectors:
       $$ v = \sum_{b \in B} c_b b, $$ where all but finitely many scalars
       $c_b \in F$ are zero. The requirement of finiteness ensures that
       algebraic sums remain strictly well-defined even when the basis $B$ is
       uncountably infinite."
Id: "a7868925-5a9a-4635-9b82-e9953ac0dd20"


Theorem:
given:
. F is \field
. V is \vector.space:over{F}
. S \:(linearly.independent)::subset:/ V
. T \:subset:/ V
where: \span:of{T}:in{V} = V
then:
. exists: B is \set
  suchThat:
  . B is? \basis:for{V}
  . S \:subset?:/ B
. exists: C is \set
  suchThat:
  . C is? \basis:for{V}
  . C \:subset?:/ T
Documented:
. called: "Basis Extension Theorem"
. description: "Every linearly independent subset extends to a basis, and every
                spanning subset contains a basis."
Proof: """
Let $V$ be a vector space over a field $F$.

1. **Every spanning set contains a basis**:
   Let $T \subseteq V$ be a spanning set: $\operatorname{span}(T) = V$.
   If $T = \emptyset$, then $V = \{0\}$ and $\emptyset \subseteq T$ is the basis.
   Otherwise, partially order the collection $\mathcal{F} = \{X \subseteq T : X \text{ is linearly independent}\}$
   by set inclusion. Every chain in $\mathcal{F}$ has an upper bound (the union of the chain,
   which is linearly independent because linear dependence involves only finitely many vectors).
   By Zorn's Lemma, $\mathcal{F}$ contains a maximal element $C$.
   If $\operatorname{span}(C) \neq V$, since $\operatorname{span}(T) = V$, there exists some $t \in T$
   not belonging to $\operatorname{span}(C)$. But then $C \cup \{t\} \subseteq T$ is linearly independent,
   contradicting the maximality of $C$.
   Thus $\operatorname{span}(C) = V$, proving that $C$ is a basis for $V$ contained in $T$.

2. **Every linearly independent set extends to a basis**:
   Let $S \subseteq V$ be linearly independent.
   Partially order the collection $\mathcal{G} = \{Y \subseteq V : S \subseteq Y \text{ and } Y \text{ is linearly independent}\}$
   by set inclusion. By Zorn's Lemma, $\mathcal{G}$ possesses a maximal element $B$.
   If $\operatorname{span}(B) \neq V$, there exists $v \in V \setminus \operatorname{span}(B)$.
   Then $B \cup \{v\}$ is linearly independent and contains $S$, contradicting the maximality of $B$.
   Thus $\operatorname{span}(B) = V$, proving that $B$ is a basis for $V$ extending $S$.
"""
Id: "c1009158-a56f-4c29-8006-7e3a6bcc662d"


TextTheorem: "The Invariance of Dimension. Any two bases of a vector space $V$
              over a field $F$ have the same cardinality. This unique cardinal
              number is the *dimension* $\dim_F(V)$.

              *Proof Sketch (Finite Dimensions)*: The result rests on the
              *Steinitz Exchange Lemma*: if $\{u_1, \dots, u_m\}$ is linearly
              independent in $V$ and $\{w_1, \dots, w_n\}$ spans $V$, then
              $m \leq n$, and one can successively exchange $m$ vectors from
              $\{w_j\}$ with $\{u_i\}$ such that the resulting set still spans
              $V$. If $B_1$ and $B_2$ are two bases of sizes $n_1$ and $n_2$,
              then $B_1$ is independent and $B_2$ spans, so $n_1 \leq n_2$.
              Symmetrically, $B_2$ is independent and $B_1$ spans, so
              $n_2 \leq n_1$. Hence $n_1 = n_2$.

              Consequently, every linearly independent set in an $n$-dimensional
              space has cardinality at most $n$, and every spanning set has
              cardinality at least $n$."
Id: "79b4b540-b701-47fc-a9b7-aa0f1e1f3a46"


Text: "The Rank-Nullity Theorem establishes that any linear map $T : V \to W$
       satisfies $\dim(V) = \dim(\ker T) + \dim(\operatorname{im} T)$.
       Geometrically, the dimension of the domain is partitioned into the
       degrees of freedom destroyed (collapsed into the kernel) and the degrees
       of freedom preserved (mapped into the image)."
Id: "d262c022-54b1-4327-b52c-8ebc6d323f34"


SectionTitle: "Coordinate Representations"
Id: "3756ca2b-b124-4d4d-8488-06685b6d23d8"


TextDefinition: "For an ordered basis $\mathcal{B} = (b_1, \dots, b_n)$ of a vector space $V$,
                 the *coordinate isomorphism* $[\,\cdot\,]_{\mathcal{B}} : V \to F^n$ maps
                 each vector $v = \sum_{i=1}^n c_i b_i$ to its unique column vector of coordinates:
                 $$ [v]_{\mathcal{B}} = \begin{pmatrix} c_1 \\ \vdots \\ c_n \end{pmatrix} \in F^n. $$
                 The coordinate vector $[v]_{\mathcal{B}}$ depends heavily on the chosen basis,
                 while the vector $v$ remains an invariant geometric entity."
Id: "eb390519-730b-45ef-a8c5-75ed6e82ba01"


TextTheorem: "Let $T : V \to V$ be a linear operator on a finite-dimensional
              space $V$. If $A = [T]_{\mathcal{B}}$ and
              $A' = [T]_{\mathcal{B}'}$ represent $T$ with respect to two
              ordered bases $\mathcal{B}$ and $\mathcal{B}'$, then:
              $$ A' = P^{-1} A P, $$ where
              $P = [I]_{\mathcal{B}'}^{\mathcal{B}}$ is the invertible
              change-of-basis matrix.

              Two matrices $A, A'$ related by $A' = P^{-1}AP$ are called
              *similar*. All similarity invariants—including the determinant
              $\det(T)$, trace $\operatorname{tr}(T)$, characteristic polynomial
              $\chi_T(t)$, minimal polynomial $m_T(t)$, and eigenvalues with
              algebraic and geometric multiplicities—are intrinsic properties of
              the linear transformation $T$, completely independent of
              coordinate choices."
Id: "5e912a97-68ed-490b-aeb4-06145687fdf4"


SectionTitle: "Dual Spaces and Annihilators"
Id: "9c48c173-2660-4181-9104-36cae5648a4d"


TextDefinition: "The *dual space* $V^* = \operatorname{Hom}_F(V, F) = \mathcal{L}(V, F)$ is
                 the vector space of all linear functionals $\varphi : V \to F$.
                 
                 If $\mathcal{B} = (b_1, \dots, b_n)$ is a basis of $V$, its *dual basis*
                 $\mathcal{B}^* = (b^1, \dots, b^n)$ of $V^*$ is uniquely determined by:
                 $$ b^i(b_j) = \delta_{ij} = \begin{cases} 1 & \text{if } i = j, \\ 0 & \text{if } i \neq j. \end{cases} $$
                 The functional $b^i$ extracts the $i$-th coordinate of a vector relative to $\mathcal{B}$.
                 
                 For finite-dimensional spaces, $\dim(V^*) = \dim(V)$, so $V \cong V^*$.
                 Furthermore, the evaluation map $\operatorname{ev} : V \to V^{**}$ into the
                 double dual (bidual), defined by $\operatorname{ev}(v)(\varphi) = \varphi(v)$,
                 is a *canonical* (basis-independent) isomorphism when $\dim(V) < \infty$,
                 rendering finite-dimensional vector spaces reflexive."
Id: "3370ae35-7b9f-4983-bba5-cc28277c31e8"


TextDefinition: "For any subspace $U \leq V$, its *annihilator* is the subspace of $V^*$
                 consisting of all linear functionals that vanish identically on $U$:
                 $$ U^\circ = \{\varphi \in V^* : \varphi(u) = 0 \text{ for all } u \in U\}. $$
                 
                 Annihilators reverse inclusions: if $U_1 \subseteq U_2$, then $U_2^\circ \subseteq U_1^\circ$.
                 When $V$ is finite-dimensional, the dimension formula holds:
                 $$ \dim(U) + \dim(U^\circ) = \dim(V). $$
                 Through the canonical isomorphism $V \cong V^{**}$, the double annihilator
                 satisfies $(U^\circ)^\circ = U$."
Id: "ab2bb4d2-ff04-4f40-a5d3-63a58c7ae9cb"


TextDefinition: "For any linear transformation $T : V \to W$, the *dual map* (or transpose)
                 $T^* : W^* \to V^*$ is defined by precomposition:
                 $$ T^*(\psi) = \psi \circ T \quad \text{for all } \psi \in W^*. $$
                 Duality reverses the direction of arrows: a measurement functional on the
                 target space $W$ pulls back to a measurement on the domain $V$.
                 In finite dimensions, the matrix of $T^*$ relative to dual bases is precisely
                 the transpose of the matrix of $T$: $[T^*]_{\mathcal{C}^*}^{\mathcal{B}^*} = ([T]_{\mathcal{B}}^{\mathcal{C}})^T$."
Id: "ebcf6beb-7c81-4a34-9fb1-ba69774d93f0"


TextTheorem: "The Fundamental Subspace Duality Theorem. For any linear map $T : V \to W$
              between finite-dimensional vector spaces:
              $$ \ker(T^*) = (\operatorname{im} T)^\circ \quad \text{and} \quad \operatorname{im}(T^*) = (\ker T)^\circ. $$
              
              *Proof*:
              1. A functional $\psi \in W^*$ satisfies $\psi \in \ker(T^*)$ if and only if
                 $T^*(\psi) = \psi \circ T = 0$. This means $\psi(T(v)) = 0$ for all $v \in V$,
                 which by definition states that $\psi$ annihilates $\operatorname{im}(T)$.
                 Thus $\ker(T^*) = (\operatorname{im} T)^\circ$.
              2. Applying dimensions: $\dim(\operatorname{im} T^*) = \dim(W^*) - \dim(\ker T^*) = \dim(W) - \dim((\operatorname{im} T)^\circ) = \dim(\operatorname{im} T)$.
                 Hence $\operatorname{rank}(T^*) = \operatorname{rank}(T)$ (row rank equals column rank).
                 Since $T^*(\psi)(\ker T) = \psi(T(\ker T)) = \psi(0) = 0$, we have
                 $\operatorname{im}(T^*) \subseteq (\ker T)^\circ$.
                 Since both subspaces have dimension $\dim(V) - \dim(\ker T) = \operatorname{rank}(T)$,
                 they are equal: $\operatorname{im}(T^*) = (\ker T)^\circ$.
                 
              This theorem forms the structural core of the Fredholm Alternative for linear
              systems: $Ax = b$ has a solution if and only if $b$ is annihilated by every
              solution $y$ to the homogeneous dual system $y^T A = 0$."
Id: "b00d626c-66d6-4368-aab5-aec10ee2a364"
